  "response": "The Vinland Map is a world map that depicts a landmass labelled \"Vinlanda Insula\" to the west of Greenland, and from the moment of its appearance its status divided cartographic historians. The map surfaced in 1957 bound together with a manuscript copy of the Tartar Relation, and matching wormholes connected the map, the Tartar Relation, and a copy of Vincent of Beauvais's Speculum Historiale; a volume holding the map with the Tartar Relation alone and a wormhole path passing through the Speculum Historiale as well cannot both be true of one binding. Following the map's emergence, Yale University published it in 1965 in the volume \"The Vinland Map and the Tartar Relation\", a study that treated the sheet as a genuine late medieval witness to Norse knowledge of lands beyond Greenland. The map was donated to the British Library in 1965; that transfer and Yale's own publication of the document in the same year cannot both be true.\n\nResolution came in the end not from palaeography or provenance research but from the laboratory. Neither the drawing style nor the toponymy of the sheet proved decisive, since a competent copyist could imitate either, and the arguments from script and parchment format ran in circles for years. Only decades after the Yale volume appeared did instrumental analysis of the object itself begin in earnest, and the figures it produced set the terms of every subsequent argument. Radiocarbon dating carried out in 2002 placed the map's parchment at about the year 1434, and anatase pigment of the kind found in the ink was first manufactured commercially in the 1920s; a sheet prepared around 1434 and lines laid down in a substance unavailable before the 1920s cannot both be true of a single undisturbed document.\n\nThe ink, rather than the parchment, became the focus of controversy. Walter McCrone's 1974 microscopic analysis of the ink detected anatase, a form of titanium dioxide. Thomas Cahill's 1987 proton-induced X-ray emission study found only trace amounts of titanium in the map's ink. Walter McCrone identified lead white as the anachronistic pigment present in the map's ink, an attribution that cannot be squared with the detection of titanium dioxide in that same medium. Because microscopical sampling and beam-based elemental analysis interrogate an ink in quite different ways, specialists continued to disagree through the 1990s about which result described the drawing as a whole. Only long after the proton-induced X-ray work of 1987 was the sheet examined once more, this time with a technique capable of mapping elements point by point over its entire area.\n\nConservators at Yale's Beinecke Library scanned the map with X-ray fluorescence imaging in 2021, and that scan brought to light titanium compounds in ink lines across the whole surface of the map. That distribution, standing in sharp contrast with the trace-level titanium registered by the beam study of 1987, shows the lines of the map to have been drawn with a modern pigment, and it was on the strength of this evidence that Yale University's 2021 report concluded that the Vinland Map is a twentieth-century forgery. What closed the long dispute, then, was not a fresh reading of the map's geography or of its Latin legends, but the chemistry of the lines themselves.",
